<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biskra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biskra is an oasis city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, on the southern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aures-mountains">Aurès Mountains</a> at the gateway to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Known in Roman times as Vescera, it lay on routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast and the desert. Near it, in 682 or 683, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kusaila">Kusaila</a> ambushed and killed the Arab commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqba-ibn-nafi">Uqba ibn Nafi</a>, a setback for the early Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biskra.jpg" alt="Biskra" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yelles · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dattes_de_Deglet_nour_%C3%A0_Tolga_(Wilaya_de_Biskra).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
